What was New Imperialism in the 19th century, and how did it differ from earlier trade-focused expansion?19th century European expansion into Asia/Africa driven by Industrial Revolution needs for raw materials and markets, distinct from earlier trade-focused expansion
What is the colonial system?Administrative frameworks established by European powers to exploit natural resources and maintain control over subject territories
What was India under British Raj?British colonial rule over Indian subcontinent from 1800s-1947, combining direct and indirect administration
What was the nature of European colonial domination in Southeast Asia by 1900, including the roles of strategic ports, plantation economies, and collaboration with local elites?European domination of Southeast Asia by 1900 through strategic ports, plantation economies, and local elite collaboration
What was the rapid partition and colonization of Africa during the 1880s to 1900, known as the "Scramble for Africa"?Rapid partition and colonization of Africa 1880s-1900, known as the "Scramble for Africa"
What is anticolonialism, and how did indigenous resistance movements and emerging nationalist responses challenge European colonial rule?Indigenous resistance movements and emerging nationalist responses to European colonial rule
What was the Sepoy Rebellion of 1857 and what triggered it?Mutiny of Indian troops triggered by Enfield rifle cartridges violating Hindu and Muslim religious beliefs
What was the Scramble for Africa and what triggered the rapid European partition of Africa between the 1880s and 1900?Rapid European partition of Africa 1880s-1900 triggered by competition and King Leopold's Congo claim
What was the Berlin Conference of 1884?Meeting establishing rules for African colonization to prevent European wars
What was the British Nile strategy and its purpose?British effort to control Nile Valley to protect Suez Canal from French threat
What is Ethiopian independence and how was it achieved?Ethiopia remained independent after defeating Italy at Battle of Adowa 1896
What was the South Africa conflict involving the Boers, British, and Zulus?Three-way struggle between Boers, British, and Zulus for control of southern Africa
What is collaboration debate, particularly regarding the choice between resisting colonizers or cooperating for pragmatic reasons that divided Asian and African elites?Choice between resisting colonizers or cooperating for pragmatic reasons dividing Asian/African elites
What is the colonial impact?Complex legacy with both destructive exploitation and unintended modernizing effects
Who was Cecil Rhodes and what were his goals?British empire builder who wanted to extend British rule worldwide and make wars impossible
Who was King Leopold II and what was his role in the Congo?Belgian king whose personal Congo claim triggered scramble, used brutal forced labor for rubber
Who was Thomas Babington Macaulay and what role did he play in British India?British official who created English-educated Indian elite "interpreters" between rulers and ruled
Who was David Livingstone and what did he advocate?Scottish missionary who opposed slavery and advocated "3 Cs": Christianity, Commerce, Civilization
Who was Muhammad Ali, the Ottoman officer known for modernizing Egypt, building the Suez Canal, and threatening the Ottoman Empire?Ottoman officer who modernized Egypt, built Suez Canal, threatened Ottoman Empire
Who was Menelik II and what were his significant contributions?Ethiopian emperor who modernized army and preserved independence at Battle of Adowa
Who was Alfred de Lesseps and what is he known for?French entrepreneur who built Suez Canal 1869, left Egypt bankrupt
Who was General Charles Gordon and what is he known for?British general killed by Mahdi at Khartoum 1885, symbol of imperial overreach
Who was Jules Ferry and what warning did he give about abstinence in relation to France's status as a power?French politician who warned abstinence would make France a third-rate power
Who was Karl Pearson and what was his belief regarding superior and inferior races?British social Darwinist who argued superior races must supplant inferior ones
Who was Rudyard Kipling, and how did he justify imperialism in his work "White Man's Burden"?Poet who justified imperialism as "White Man's Burden" to civilize "half-devil" peoples
Who was Edmund Morel and what is he known for?British journalist who exposed brutal exploitation in Belgian Congo with "Black Man's Burden"
Who was Henry Curzon, the British official who described the empire as the "greatest instrument for good under Providence"?British official who called empire "greatest instrument for good under Providence"
Who was Albert Sarrault and what role did he play in justifying colonialism?French governor who justified colonialism as redistributing earth's wealth for universal life
Who was Eduard Douwes Dekker?Dutch official who exposed Javanese exploitation in "Max Havelaar"
Who was Ram Mohan Roy and what was his contribution to Indian society?Bengali brahmin who founded Brahmo Samaj, first stirrings of Indian nationalism
What was the Industrial Revolution?19th century mechanization and factory production
What is Suez?suez
What is brit_egypt?brit_egypt
What is the Mahdi?mahdi
What is union_south_africa?union_south_africa
What is racial segregation?racial_segregation
What is the year when Stamford Raffles founded Singapore?Stamford Raffles founded Singapore
What happened in 1826 involving British actions in Lower Burma?British attacked lower Burma
What significant event related to the British rail network occurred in northern India in 1853?British rail network opened in northern India
What was the Sepoy Rebellion of 1857?Sepoy Rebellion
What happened in 1858 involving France and Vietnam?France attacked Vietnam
What was created by the French in 1859?French created Indochina Union
What was established in 1863 regarding Cambodia?French protectorate over Cambodia
What significant event occurred in 1869 related to the Suez Canal?Suez Canal completed
What significant event happened in 1873 related to the Zanzibar slave market?Zanzibar slave market closed
What is the significance of the year 1874 in relation to the British and the Gold Coast colony?British established Gold Coast colony
What was the British informal protectorate over Egypt established in 1881?British informal protectorate over Egypt
What was the Berlin Conference on Africa in 1884?Berlin Conference on Africa
What significant event involving the Mahdi and General Gordon occurred in 1885?Mahdi captured Khartoum killing General Gordon
What was established in 1893 as the French protectorate over Laos?French protectorate over Laos
What happened in 1896 involving Britain, France, Thailand, and Ethiopia?Britain and France agreed to neutralize Thailand; Ethiopia won at Adowa
What significant events occurred in 1898?Commodore Dewey defeated Spanish fleet in Manila Bay; US took Philippines; Fashoda Incident
What significant event began in 1899?Boer War began
What was the Indochinese Union created by the French in 1900?French created Indochinese Union
In what year did the Boer War end?Boer War ended
What is the Casement Commission report on the Belgian Congo from 1904?Casement Commission report on Belgian Congo
What significant event occurred in 1910 related to the Union of South Africa?Union of South Africa established
What caused the new imperialism in the context of the Industrial Revolution?The Industrial Revolution created insatiable demand for raw materials and export markets beyond domestic capacity
What was the economic motive behind new imperialism?European economies required colonies to prevent depression by absorbing surplus manufactured goods and supplying cheap materials
What was the political motive behind new imperialism?Social Darwinism convinced Europeans that national survival depended on proving fitness through colonial expansion
What was the moral justification of the "White Man's Burden" in the context of new imperialism?"White Man's Burden" to spread Christianity, democracy, progress and rescue "primitive" peoples from poverty and disease
What was Cecil Rhodes' vision in the context of new imperialism?Secret society to extend British rule worldwide including Africa, Holy Land, South America, and even recover United States
In the context of new imperialism, how did tactics differ from earlier periods?Shift from trade footholds and coastal presence to direct territorial control and inland penetration after 1880
What technological advantages enabled conquest in the context of new imperialism?Steam navigation, quinine for malaria, Maxim guns and Gatling guns allowing small European forces to defeat larger armies
What was the timeline of intensification in the context of new imperialism?After 1880 competition became a "scramble" as all major powers plus US and Japan engaged in global land grab
What is indirect rule in the context of the colonial system?Governing through local elites who maintained authority in exchange for economic rewards and confirmation of status, used in princely states and Dutch East Indies
In the context of the colonial system, what is direct rule?Removing local elites and replacing with officials from mother country, used where resistance was strong like Burma and some African territories
In the context of the colonial system, what is assimilation?French policy to transform colonial societies into Western image with European institutions, theoretically offering equality
In the context of the colonial system, what is meant by "association"?British preference for preserving local traditions while collaborating with elites, treating subjects as culturally distinct
What philosophical justification underpinned the colonial system, particularly through Social Darwinism providing pseudoscientific validity to "might makes right" and racial hierarchy?Social Darwinism providing pseudoscientific validity to "might makes right" and racial hierarchy
What educational reforms were introduced in India under the British Raj?British civil service examination, schools for Indian elites, first female medical student 1875, expanded girls' education for better wives/mothers
What social reforms were enacted in India under the British Raj?Sati outlawed, widows permitted remarriage, thuggee brigandage suppressed
What infrastructure was built in India under the British Raj?Railroads, telegraph, postal service introduced shortly after Britain, Calcutta-Delhi highway 1839, northern India rail network 1853
What economic developments occurred in India under the British Raj, particularly regarding textile and jute manufacturing and industrialization?Limited textile and jute manufacturing, 80 mills in Bombay by 1920s, but prevented broader industrialization
What was the economic cost of British rule in India, particularly regarding the impact on the Bengali textile industry and the zamindar tax system?British textiles destroyed Bengali textile industry, zamindar tax system displaced peasants and increased rural debt
What was the political cost of British rule in India, considering that no democratic institutions were introduced, as reflected in the quote: "democratic institutions can no more be carried to India by Englishmen than they can carry ice in their luggage"?No democratic institutions introduced, quote: democratic institutions "can no more be carried to India by Englishmen than they can carry ice in their luggage"
What was the psychological impact of British rule on Indians during the British Raj?British arrogance wounded Indian pride, educated Indians developed confused cultural loyalties, "Indian in blood, English in taste"
What was Thomas Macaulay's policy in the context of India under the British Raj?Created English-educated elite class to serve as interpreters between British and millions they governed
What territories did France control in Southeast Asia during the colonial period?Indochina Union: Vietnam (attacked 1858), Cambodia (1863), Laos (1893) - southern Vietnam direct rule, northern protectorate
In the context of Southeast Asia colonial history, what territories did Britain control?Malaya, Singapore (founded 1819 by Stamford Raffles), Burma (attacked 1826) - Singapore was strategic port for China trade
What territory did the Netherlands control in Southeast Asia during the colonial period, and how did they govern it?Dutch East Indies (early 1600s) with indirect rule through indigenous aristocracy collecting taxes
In the context of Southeast Asia colonial history, what territories did Spain and the United States control?Philippines (Spanish 1521, US after 1898 Spanish-American War) as stepping-stone to China trade
Which country remained independent in Southeast Asia during the colonial period, and how was this achieved?Thailand through astute diplomacy of Kings Mongkut and Chulalongkorn, neutralized as buffer zone 1896
What was the primary goal of colonial powers in Southeast Asia?Economic profit through cash crop exports: teakwood, rubber, tin, spices, tea, coffee, palm oil, sugar, copra
What labor system was used in Southeast Asia during the colonial period?Plantation agriculture with forced recruitment ("shanghaied"), inhumane conditions, high death rates
In the context of Southeast Asia colonialism, what was the administrative approach used?Indirect rule preferred as less costly and culturally less corrosive, working with local collaborators
What were the democratic reforms in Southeast Asia during the colonial period, including the role of legislative councils, the inclusion of indigenous elites, and concerns about educated unemployed rebels?Legislative councils initially European-only, slow inclusion of conservative indigenous elites, fear of creating educated unemployed rebels
What was the population impact of improved sanitation during Southeast Asia's colonial period?Improved sanitation caused dramatic growth (Java 1M to 40M) leading to rural poverty and urban migration
In the context of African colonial history, what preceded formal colonization?Decline of Atlantic slave trade (illegal in Britain 1808, US 1863) replaced by "legitimate trade" in peanuts, timber, palm oil
In the context of African colonial history, where were early settlements established?British Gold Coast and Sierra Leone for freed slaves, Liberia with US assistance, French Senegal for peanut plantations
In the context of African colonial history, what was an informal empire, such as areas like Egypt and Lower Nigeria under loose protectorates without formal annexation before the 1880s?Areas like Egypt, lower Nigeria under loose protectorates without formal annexation before 1880s
What was British indirect rule in the context of African colonialism?Preserving African political traditions through local chiefs with British district officers as intermediaries, used in Nigeria
In the context of African colonialism, what was French direct rule?Centralized system with governor-general, commissioners, African administrators required to be conversant in French
What was the French assimilation policy in the context of African colonialism?Goal to assimilate Africans into French culture theoretically offering equality and participation in French politics
In the context of African colonial history, what was high colonialism, particularly in the post-World War I phase characterized as a "sacred trust" with more social services but increased segregation and race consciousness?Post-WWI phase as "sacred trust" with more social services but increased segregation and race consciousness
What was the impact of colonialism on women in Africa?Mixed: ended forced marriage/genital mutilation, but European gender attitudes restricted women's traditional roles
In the context of anticolonialism, what was traditional resistance?Initial defense of traditional institutions by existing ruling classes and peasant revolts
In the context of anticolonialism, what forms did resistance take?Guerrilla warfare, religious movements, peasant uprisings, collaboration vs resistance dilemmas
What was the path to nationalism in the context of anticolonialism, considering factors such as colonial borders creating artificial nations, the spread of Western ideas of citizenship, and the unification of elites through resentment of racial superiority?Colonial borders created artificial nations, Western ideas of citizenship spread, resentment of racial superiority unified elites
What was the role of education in anticolonialism?Western-educated lawyers, teachers, journalists became vanguard of nationalist movements
What was the Sepoy Rebellion in the context of anticolonialism?Largest uprising against European authority in mid-19th century, defeated but frightened British into reforms
What was the Mahdi Revolt in the context of anticolonialism?Sudanese religious resistance that temporarily defeated British at Khartoum 1885
What was the Ashanti resistance in the context of anticolonialism?West African struggle against British with broad popular support but ultimately defeated
What was the Can Vuong movement in the context of anticolonialism?Vietnamese "Save the King" organization continuing resistance after monarchy surrendered
What triggered the Sepoy Rebellion of 1857?New Enfield rifle cartridges greased with cow fat and pig lard violated religious prohibitions requiring biting cartridge
Why did the Sepoy Rebellion of 1857 fail?Lacked clear goals, Hindu-Muslim discord prevented coordination, British forces supplemented by loyalist Sikh and Gurkha units
What were the consequences of the Sepoy Rebellion of 1857?Reduced proportion of Indian troops, preference for "loyal" ethnic groups, final suppression of Mughal Dynasty, transferred administration to British Crown
What triggered the Scramble for Africa?King Leopold II of Belgium claimed Congo for personal enrichment under guise of missionary activity
In the context of the Scramble for Africa, what was the economic trigger?Discovery of gold and diamonds in Transvaal, copper in Belgian Congo
In the context of the Scramble for Africa, what was the strategic trigger related to the need to protect the Suez Canal as a lifeline to India?Need to protect Suez Canal (completed 1869) as lifeline to India
What technological factor enabled the Scramble for Africa?Quinine discovery dramatically reduced European mortality in tropical Africa
What principles were established at the Berlin Conference of 1884?Effective occupation required for recognition, free commerce in Congo and Niger, further efforts to end slave trade
What was the outcome of the Berlin Conference of 1884, considering that no African delegates were present and the rules recognized the inevitability of imperialism while trying to manage competition?No African delegates present, rules recognized inevitability of imperialism while trying to manage competition
What was the Fashoda Incident in the context of the Berlin Conference of 1884?1898 confrontation between Britain and France in Sudan, French backed down confirming British Nile control
Why was Egypt crucial in the context of British Nile strategy?Muhammad Ali's modernization, canal construction left Egypt bankrupt and vulnerable to British financial control 1881
What was the Mahdi Revolt in the context of the British Nile strategy?1881-85 Sudanese religious uprising led by Muhammad Ahmad defeating General Gordon at Khartoum
What was the outcome of the British Nile strategy?British consolidated control over entire Nile Valley after Mahdi threat and Fashoda
How did Ethiopia achieve its independence?Emperor Menelik modernized army and purchased modern European weapons
What was the battle that secured Ethiopian independence by destroying the Italian invasion force in 1896?Battle of Adowa 1896 destroyed Italian invasion force
What was the significance of Ethiopia being the only African nation besides Liberia to preserve its independence during the colonial era?Only African nation besides Liberia to preserve independence in colonial era
In the context of the South African conflict, who were the Boers?Afrikaans-speaking descendants of Dutch settlers who migrated east in Great Trek 1830s
Why did the Boers migrate during the South African conflict?To escape British abolition of slavery 1834 and British sympathy for African rights
What was the Zulu resistance in the context of the South African conflict?Bantu people led by Shaka fought bitter wars against Boers and British, subdued by century's end
What caused the Boer War in South Africa?Cecil Rhodes attempted to seize Transvaal's gold/diamonds, war 1899-1902 ended with British victory
What was the outcome of the South Africa conflict?Union of South Africa 1910 with white-only self-government, divided between English and Afrikaner populations
What was Hoang Cao Khai's argument in the context of the collaboration debate?Resistance is futile, causes unnecessary suffering, should accept reality for people's sake
In the context of collaborative debate, what was Phan Dinh Phung's response regarding loyalty and resistance?Loyalty to king and country based on five moral obligations, resistance is righteous duty
What was Ram Mohan Roy's approach in the context of collaboration debate?Bengali brahmin who founded Brahmo Samaj to defend Hindu values while accepting useful European ideas
What were the positive effects of colonial impact?Modern infrastructure, global trade networks, democratic ideas introduced, women's rights awareness, potential for future development
What were the negative effects of colonial impact?Economic exploitation, destruction of local industries, racial discrimination, prevented local development, brutally applied
What was the fundamental weakness of colonial impact, considering that it was based entirely on the self-interest of colonial powers, subordinated the needs of colonial peoples, and prevented self-determination?Based entirely on self-interest of colonial powers, colonial peoples' needs always subordinate, prevented self-determination
What was the tragedy of the colonial impact on age-old societies under the false "civilizing mission"?Age-old societies squeezed of resources under false "civilizing mission," suffering for nothing rather than through colonial era
What were the consequences of new imperialism?colonial impact
What were the consequences of new imperialism on the colonial system?colonial system
In which region was the colonial system implemented under the British Raj?india under british raj
In which regions was the colonial system implemented, specifically in Southeast Asia?southeast asia colonial
In which regions was the colonial system implemented?africa colonial
What event did British rule in India cause in 1857?sepoy rebellion 1857
What did India generate under the British Raj?sepoy rebellion 1857
How was colonialism in Africa manifested during the Scramble for Africa?scramble for africa
What does anticolonialism exemplify: collaboration or debate?collaboration debate
What did the Sepoy Rebellion of 1857 lead to?anticolonialism
What regulated the Scramble for Africa?berlin conference 1884
What motivated the Scramble for Africa, including the British Nile strategy?british nile strategy
What did the Scramble for Africa fail to overcome?ethiopian independence
How did the Scramble for Africa manifest in the South African conflict?south africa conflict
What triggered the Scramble for Africa?king leopold ii
What did the Berlin Conference of 1884 attempt to regulate during the Scramble for Africa?scramble for africa
What does the British Nile strategy require regarding the Suez?suez
What did Ethiopian independence demonstrate?anticolonialism
What did the South Africa conflict produce?union_south_africa
What did the Industrial Revolution cause in relation to new imperialism?new imperialism
What motivated the Suez Crisis involving Britain and Egypt?brit_egypt
What did Brit_Egypt provoke?mahdi
What did the Mahdi inspire?anticolonialism
